Q: How to get max length of a property using Entity Framework Core A: <p>Reflection is the most straighforward choice, but it will give you in many cases the wrong answer. You are interested in getting the actual limitations that Entity Framework has set up for columns in your DbContext's <em>model</em>.</p>
<ul>
<li>Reflection is an easy and quick way to get the set maxlengths set in this question programatically</li>
<li>But in many cases there will be db contexts where you should ask the Entity Framework (core) model itself about such things - which entity type it has set up, which columns and which constraints exist</li>
<li>in this case we can as the Entity Framework Core model to find the string constrained fields</li>
</ul>
<p>About using Reflection to retrieve the max lengths set on properties/columns, this can be reliable if that is the only way string lenghts are set.</p>
<p>But in a more typical scenario, there might be constrains set up using the Fluent Api for example in <em>onmodelcreating</em> and so on.</p>
<p>I more generic recommended approach is to use the model of the <em>DbContext</em> itself.</p>
<p>I wrote these extension methods in Linqpad that achieves this.</p>
<ul>
<li>Note that I use Linqpad 8 together with Entity Framework 8 here.
That is why I cast this object to DbContext, this is just Linqpad specific.
Otherwise than that, the code below is general-purpose. (I use Linqpad 8 to test out different things in C# as a prototyping tool.)</li>
</ul>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>void Main()
{   
    var dbContext = (DbContext)this;

    Console.WriteLine(&quot;----------------- GET ALL THE ENTITY TYPE FIELDS/COLUMNS WITH A STRING LENGTH CONSTRAINT------------&quot;);

    var stringlengthConstrainedField = dbContext.GetAllStringPropertyLengths();

    foreach (var constrainedField in stringlengthConstrainedField)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($&quot;{constrainedField.EntityName} {constrainedField.PropertyName} {constrainedField.ColumnName} {constrainedField.MaxLength}&quot;);
    }


    Console.WriteLine(&quot;-------------- GET SPECIFIC ENTITY TYPE's FIELDS/COLUMS WITH A STRING LENGTH CONSTRAINT---------------&quot;);

    var constrainedFieldsInSpecificTable = dbContext.GetStringPropertyLengths&lt;Users&gt;();

    foreach (var constrainedField in constrainedFieldsInSpecificTable)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($&quot;{constrainedField.EntityName} {constrainedField.PropertyName} {constrainedField.ColumnName} {constrainedField.MaxLength}&quot;);
    }

}

public static class DbContextExtensions
{
    public static IEnumerable&lt;(string EntityName, string PropertyName, string ColumnName, int? MaxLength)&gt;
        GetAllStringPropertyLengths(this DbContext context, Type? specificEntityType = null)
    {
        var model = context.Model;

        IEntityType? specificEntity = null;

        if (specificEntityType != null){
            specificEntity = model.FindEntityType(specificEntityType);
        }

        if (specificEntityType != null &amp;&amp; specificEntity == null){
            yield break;
        }

        var entityTypes = specificEntity == null ? model.GetEntityTypes() : new[] { specificEntity };


        foreach (var entityType in entityTypes)
        {
            var clrType = entityType.ClrType;
            foreach (var property in entityType.GetProperties())
            {
                // Only interested in string properties
                if (property.ClrType != typeof(string))
                    continue;

                // Get max length from EF metadata
                var maxLength = property.GetMaxLength();

                // If no length set, skip
                if (maxLength == null)
                    continue;

                // Get property name and column name
                var propertyName = property.Name;
                var columnName = property.GetColumnName();

                yield return (clrType.Name, propertyName, columnName, maxLength);
            }
        }
    }

    public static IEnumerable&lt;(string EntityName, string PropertyName, string ColumnName, int? MaxLength)&gt;
    GetStringPropertyLengths&lt;TModel&gt;(this DbContext context)
    {
        var stringPropertyLenghtsForType = GetAllStringPropertyLengths(context, typeof(TModel));
        return stringPropertyLenghtsForType;
    }

}
</code></pre>
<p>Inside Linqpad, we can add a connection to for example an SQL Server database and &quot;Use this connection&quot;, the (this) object will be set so you can directly cast it to DbContext.</p>
<p>Otherwise, you can use the extension methods here on your DbContext to get the max length constrained fields. I have tested them and see they are working, they list up the entity types and columns in the model and the string length set on them. We will only list up the properties that are in the model in a entity type that are of type string and look at the ones that actually has got a string length set. Otherwise, they are most likely nvarchar(max) fields or varchar(max) if you have decided to use that db column type for string-based fields with unconstrained length.</p>
